ABSD per-port patch for sbase 0.1 find (userland/README.md, "Patches").

Failure: find links fork, execvp and waitpid for -exec and -ok,
clock_gettime for -atime/-ctime/-mtime, and (through libutil/mode.o's
getumask) umask; the kit refuses fork and exec at link time
(C-INTERFACE.md 10) and no archive defines clock_gettime or umask
(userland/DEMAND.tsv: find, unresolved clock_gettime execve fork umask
waitpid). And every file time is 0 on ABSD (C-INTERFACE.md 11, CPA-012),
so -atime/-ctime/-mtime/-newer would answer without meaning.

Change, under the controller's D4 ruling (port-local ENOSYS stubs,
in the port's own patch, disclosed here, in userland/README.md and in
the CPA catalog; never in the kit or adapter): spawn() becomes a stub
that fails with ENOSYS, and -exec and -ok are refused while the
expression is parsed, before any path is visited: status 1,
"find: -exec: cannot run commands on this system: Function not
implemented" (or -ok). Both builds, so the host reference pins the
refusal. With ABSD_NO_MTIME (the ABSD build; temporary until R3 brings
file times to C) -atime, -ctime, -mtime and -newer are refused the same
way ("find: -atime/-ctime/-mtime/-newer: file times are not available on
this system", status 1) and clock_gettime is not called (only those
primaries read the start time). With ABSD_NO_UMASK (the ABSD build) find
defines a link-only umask that returns 0 and changes nothing: find calls
parsemode(str, 0, 0) and never asks for the mask.

Preserved: the traversal is upstream's: lstat/stat, opendir, readdir and
path concatenation (no *at call; the adapter's opendir holds a snapshot
and no handle, so depth costs memory, not descriptors), loop detection by
st_dev/st_ino, and every other primary and operator. Without the ABSD
defines the time primaries behave as upstream. No adapter or kit change.

